.he "rm (1)                       8/12/26                       rm (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
rm
- remove files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
rm [ -fir ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Rm removes the named files.  A file with more than one link to it
survives the removal of any one of them; the directory entry goes and
the file itself goes when the last entry does.
.sp
Removing a file needs write permission on the directory it is in, not
on the file.  Rm asks before removing a file the user cannot write,
unless -f is given.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-f	Remove without asking, whatever the permissions.

.ti -10
-i	Ask before every file.

.ti -10
-r	Descend into directories and remove their contents.

.in -10
.sp
-r is the one to be careful with: it removes the directory and
everything beneath it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Rm refuses to remove "..", which stops the commonest way of destroying
a tree from inside it by mistake.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), mkdir (1)
